{%- comment -%}
  Category tiles — a FULL-BLEED MOSAIC, committed to.

  The gap is now exactly 0, not the theme's 2px --gap. 1-2px between
  photographs does not read as a deliberate mosaic: at any device pixel ratio
  it reads as a CSS bug — a hairline of page ground showing through where the
  images failed to meet. Either the tiles touch or they sit in a real grid with
  a real gutter. This section touches. --gap is overridden LOCALLY on the grid
  element only (theme.css styles this grid as `gap: var(--gap)`), so the global
  2px keeps doing its job everywhere else in the theme.

  Labels: `label_position` per block. "Over image" is the mosaic default and
  keeps the ink veil + per-tile "Label colour" escape hatch. "Below image" is
  the real fix for pale product photography — a label under the frame needs no
  veil at all, so nothing is painted over the photograph. Category shots run
  bright (pale limestone, white gravel, ivory garments) and a merchant who
  shoots on white should be able to opt out of overlay text entirely rather
  than tune a gradient.

  Labels sit on a left baseline rather than dead centre: centred type over a
  photograph has no edge to align to, so four tiles read as four unrelated
  compositions. A shared inline-start edge is what makes a row read as one row.

  Images are Shopify Files, referenced from templates/index.json with an
  empty default so each store starts with its own picks.
{%- endcomment -%}
<section class="sec--tight bleed reveal">
  {%- comment -%} --gap: 0px is set here, in markup, on purpose: it is the one
  declaration that decides whether this section reads as a mosaic or as a bug,
  and setting the variable (not `gap`) leaves any `gap` rule the stylesheet
  writes for .home-cats still able to win. {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="home-cats home-cats--flush" style="--gap: 0px;">
    {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign col = block.settings.collection
        assign url = block.settings.link
        if url == blank and col != blank
          assign url = col.url
        endif
        comment
          A merchant-entered tile label wins in every language, falling back to
          the collection's own (Shopify-localized) title. The priority used to
          flip on non-primary locales, which discarded the typed label there.
        endcomment
        assign label = block.settings.label | default: col.title
        assign label_pos = block.settings.label_position | default: 'over'
      -%}
      <a
        class="home-cat{% if block.settings.label_color == 'ink' %} home-cat--ink{% endif %}"
        data-label="{{ label_pos }}"
        href="{{ url | default: routes.all_products_collection_url }}"
        {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
      >
        <span class="home-cat__frame">
          {%- if block.settings.image != blank -%}
            {%- comment -%} PERFORMANCE: at a flat width ladder a
            phone downloaded FOUR tiles at 780w / ~330 KB each — 1.3 MB of
            navigation furniture, one of the largest avoidable page-weight
            costs on this template. A <picture> caps the ceiling per viewport (640w phone / 900w
            desktop) instead of under-declaring `sizes`, so the browser still gets
            honest layout information. These tiles carry a type label over a soft
            background; ~1.5× device pixels is invisible on them. {%- endcomment -%}
            <picture>
              <source
                media="(min-width: 721px)"
                srcset="{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w, {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 760 }} 760w, {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w"
                sizes="50vw">
              <img
                src="{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 560 }}"
                srcset="{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w, {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 560 }} 560w, {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 640 }} 640w"
                sizes="100vw"
                width="{{ block.settings.image.width }}" height="{{ block.settings.image.height }}"
                alt="" loading="{% if forloop.index <= 2 %}eager{% else %}lazy{% endif %}" decoding="async">
            </picture>
          {%- else -%}
            {%- comment -%} Vary the placeholder per tile. Four tiles all drawing
            the SAME line illustration reads as a rendering fault rather than as
            four categories waiting for photographs — which is exactly the
            impression a merchant forms in the first minute after choosing a
            layout. Shopify ships collection-1..6; product-card.liquid already
            cycles its own the same way. {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign ph_n = forloop.index0 | modulo: 6 | plus: 1 -%}
            {{ 'collection-' | append: ph_n | placeholder_svg_tag }}
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%} Bottom scrim, only under an overlay label — a tile whose
          label sits below the frame has nothing to protect and must not be
          darkened. Its own element rather than a background on the label, so the
          veil can run the full width of the tile while the type stays on the
          gutter edge: a veil sized to the word is exactly the "dark smudge behind
          one word" look the radial version had. {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if label_pos == 'over' -%}
            <span class="home-cat__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%} The label is inside the frame for BOTH positions so the
          two layouts share one box: over the image it is absolutely positioned
          against this frame, below it flows after the picture. Nothing moves in
          the DOM when a merchant flips the setting. {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="home-cat__label"><span class="dl home-cat__name">{{ label | escape }}</span></span>
        </span>
      </a>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
</section>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Category tiles",
  "max_blocks": 8,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "category",
      "name": "Category",
      "settings": [
        { "type": "text", "id": "label", "label": "Label", "info": "Rendered uppercase. Falls back to the collection title." },
        { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Image", "info": "Shopify Files: brand-cat-*.webp (portrait 2:3)." },
        { "type": "collection", "id": "collection", "label": "Collection" },
        { "type": "url", "id": "link", "label": "Link override", "info": "Leave empty to link to the collection." },
        {
          "type": "select", "id": "label_position", "label": "Label position", "default": "over",
          "info": "Below the image if the photograph is too pale to carry type — no shading is painted over it at all.",
          "options": [
            { "value": "over", "label": "Over image" },
            { "value": "below", "label": "Below image" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "select", "id": "label_color", "label": "Label colour", "default": "white",
          "info": "Ink for very bright images. Only applies when the label sits over the image.",
          "options": [
            { "value": "white", "label": "White" },
            { "value": "ink", "label": "Ink" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Category tiles",
      "blocks": [
        { "type": "category", "settings": { "label": "Dresses", "image": "", "collection": "womens-dresses", "label_position": "over" } },
        { "type": "category", "settings": { "label": "Coats", "image": "", "collection": "womens-coats-jackets", "label_position": "over" } },
        { "type": "category", "settings": { "label": "Knitwear", "image": "", "collection": "womens-knitwear", "label_position": "over" } },
        { "type": "category", "settings": { "label": "Tops", "image": "", "collection": "womens-tops-blouses", "label_position": "over" } }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
